new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..0cf1820327bf52f580ab7c3cf716736532d991ca
--- /dev/null
+++ b/hakopod-ownership/OWNERSHIP_PROTOCOL.md
@@ -0,0 +1,86 @@
+# Hakopod provider ownership protocol
+
+Status: patch candidate. Strict ownership is active only when the storage
+controller, pageservers, safekeepers, compute control service, and proxy are
+started with their Hakopod ownership settings. The storage controller advertises
+`hakopod-ownership-v1` only in that mode.
+
+The caller generates a cryptographically random 128-bit UUID token and persists it
+before its first provider request. It sends the token in
+`Hakopod-Ownership-Token`. The provider canonicalizes the resource kind,
+external key, and all creation parameters and hashes them with SHA-256.
+
+The storage controller persists one immutable ledger row per resource key and
+one per token. A replay with the same token and parameter hash returns the
+stored result. A different token for the same key, or the same token with a
+different hash, returns 409. Describe responses return the token. Delete
+requires the token and records a tombstone before releasing the external key.
+
+The contract covers tenant creation, timeline creation, pageserver
+registration, and safekeeper registration. Timeline creation must propagate
+the token to pageservers and safekeepers; those services must enforce the same
+key/token rule before mutation. Legacy mutation routes must be disabled while
+strict ownership mode is enabled, otherwise an actor can bypass the ledger.
+
+Tenant deletion is a three-step protocol. `validate_only=true` holds the
+controller tenant-operation lock and performs a read-only, bounded preflight
+without loading a detached tenant or changing the ledger, placement, pending
+operations, tombstones, or provider data. It validates every child ledger row,
+the matching safekeeper records on the union of current and replacement sets,
+and every remote pageserver descendant.
+
+`prepare_delete=true` repeats the preflight, transitions the tenant ledger to
+`deleting` to block new child reservations, and asks the pageserver to publish
+an immutable tenant fence outside the deletable prefix. The pageserver binds
+that fence to the exact authorized child set, rescans after publishing it, and
+returns a provider-generated retry token. The controller stores that exact
+token durably before replying. A crash after fence publication resumes by
+reading the same fence token; a conflicting token or child set returns 409.
+Hakopod must complete preparation before changing claims, compute, timelines,
+or tenant data. The final delete supplies the ownership and preparation tokens
+in headers, revalidates all remaining descendants, and then creates child
+tombstones and deletes data. Tokens never appear in request URLs.
+
+Timeline reservation uses an immutable conditional-create fence outside the
+timeline data prefix. Before publishing that fence, and again afterward, the
+pageserver lists the exact timeline prefix without a delimiter and with a
+one-key limit. Any pre-existing key rejects adoption. Tenant and timeline
+deletion fences remain outside tenant data prefixes so deletion cannot erase
+them. Strict-mode timeline creation checks the tenant fence before and after
+reservation, and the controller refuses child ledger reservation after the
+parent tenant starts deletion.
+
+An exact retry may resume timeline data written after its immutable reservation
+even when no local timeline survived the interruption. The authenticated
+controller request, matching ledger token and request hash, and matching remote
+reservation together provide authority for that recovery; a fresh reservation
+still requires an empty remote prefix. During tenant deletion, controller
+placement remains durable until both pageserver and safekeeper children are
+absent, so a restart can continue cleanup before removing the final ledger row.
+
+Compute uses the existing `ComputeSpec.operation_uuid`. Hakopod supplies the
+same durable token there and accepts a configured compute only when `/status`
+returns that exact value together with the expected tenant and timeline.
+
+## Provider state machine
+
+`reserved -> applying -> completed -> deleting -> tombstoned`
+
+`failed` is terminal and retains the key. A worker may resume `reserved` or
+`applying` only with the same token and hash. Completion stores the exact
+provider response identity in `result` before replying.
+
+## Crash windows
+
+1. Before reserve commit: no provider mutation occurred; retry may reserve.
+2. After reserve commit, before mutation: replay resumes under the same token.
+3. During mutation: the resource service checks the same token, so replay can
+   only resume or observe that token's resource.
+4. After mutation, before ledger completion: reconciliation reads the token
+   from the resource service, verifies the parameter hash and identity, then
+   completes the ledger.
+5. After completion, before response: replay returns the stored result.
+6. During delete: the tombstone fences both stale create replay and key reuse.
+
+Configuration equality, deterministic identifiers, authenticated inspection,
+and a Hakopod-local pending intent are never sufficient proof of ownership.
